#!/usr/bin/env node
// kabo-headers - the `headersHelper` the bundled MCP server is configured with (.mcp.json).
//
// The host runs this once per MCP request and reads **one line of JSON** from stdout, which it merges
// into the request headers. The contract this helper has to satisfy, exactly:
//
//   stdout on success: {"Authorization":"Bearer <jwt>"}\n   - exactly that, one line, nothing else
//   stdout on failure: 0 bytes
//
// Relay mode (`--relay`): instead of emitting the header, relay this machine's buffered
// skill-verify failures (<data root>/pending-reports.jsonl) to the platform over one authenticated
// `tools/call telemetry_report_usage`, and delete exactly the entries the server confirmed.
// stdout is ALWAYS 0 bytes in relay mode. Exit codes mirror probe mode - 0 done (nothing to relay is
// also 0), 1 the server rejected the credential, 2 could not tell (network, timeout, protocol, or a
// tool-level error), 3 the access token is expired locally (relay mode never renews either).
// stderr carries at most one line, and in relay mode it never contains the /kabo-login substring:
// the parallel probe is the authority on sign-in state, and two verdicts on one line of terminal
// would be one too many.
// The mode exists because the buffer previously had no channel of its own: SessionStart injected the
// events into the model's opening context and asked the main agent to call the tool. A model obeying
// its safety rules refuses that - injected text asking for a tool call is indistinguishable from a
// prompt-injection attack - so the queue drained only when a user explicitly authorized it, and
// because nothing could confirm a relay, the same events were re-injected every session until they
// expired. Doing it here fixes both halves: this file already holds the credential, and a tool
// result is a confirmation the buffer can be pruned against.
//
// Probe mode (`--probe`): instead of emitting the header,
// send one authenticated no-op request to this deployment's MCP path and report whether the server
// still accepts the credential. stdout is ALWAYS 0 bytes in probe mode; the outcome is the exit
// code plus one canonical stderr sentence per outcome - 0 the server accepted it, 1 sign-in
// unusable (same two stderr sentences as below; in probe mode a refresh token whose
// refresh_expires_at is already in the past is this verdict too, read off the file without a
// request and without deleting anything), 2 could not tell (network, timeout, or any unexpected
// condition; never treated as a sign-out), 3 the access token is expired locally (probe mode never
// renews, so it cannot ask the server; the next default-mode call renews it - a credential in this
// state is PRESENT and expected to work, which is why it must not share exit 2 with "unreachable").
// Exit 3 exists in probe mode only; the default mode never returns it.
// On exit 0 the probe may additionally print one informational stderr line when the refresh token
// expires within REFRESH_WARN_MS (7 days): the date, so the user can re-sign-in before a session
// starts with a dead credential. That line never changes the exit code.
// The mode exists for the SessionStart hook: the default mode trusts the local expiry timestamp,
// so a credential the server has stopped accepting (revoked family, consumed rotation) keeps being
// served until every call 401s mid-session with no explanation - the probe surfaces that state at
// session start instead, where the fix (/kabo-login) can be said out loud. Until 2026-08-23 the
// hook folded exit 2 and the locally-expired case into silence; now every outcome has a sentence,
// so the hook can always say something true.
//   exit 0  header emitted
//   exit 1  the sign-in state is unusable, with two distinct stderr lines for its two situations:
//           - no credential, or the refresh token is dead or expired -> the needs-login line,
//             which must name /kabo-login (signing in is the fix);
//           - the credential belongs to a different endpoint than this session is configured
//             for -> the endpoint-mismatch line, which must name kabo-auth status first (the
//             command that shows both endpoints) - redoing the login without fixing the
//             configuration just mints another credential that mismatches the same way
//   exit 2  the authorization server could not be reached -> a network problem, NOT a sign-in
//           problem; the credential file is left untouched
//
// The host only distinguishes zero from non-zero. The two non-zero codes exist for humans and for the
// test suite - and precisely because the host cannot tell them apart, the stderr line has to. That is
// also why exit 1 carries two different sentences rather than one: the host swallows the code, the
// sentence is all a stuck user ever sees, and "sign in again" is wrong advice for a credential that
// is merely bound to another endpoint. A user who redoes the whole login because of one flaky minute
// - or because of one mis-set KABO_API_ENDPOINT - was failed by that line.
//
// Both extra modes keep this file thin on purpose: everything that is merely protocol (the two
// JSON-RPC bodies, the tolerant response parse, the buffer read and prune) lives in
// scripts/lib/common.js, where no token ever is. What stays here is the part that cannot move: the
// header and the fetch that carries it.
//
// WARNING - this is the only file in the plugin allowed to contain the Authorization/Bearer literals.
// The token is never written to argv, env, logs or stderr; only to stdout, as one line of JSON:
//   - no token, token prefix, token length or "masked" fragment on stderr. There is deliberately no
//     masking helper anywhere in this tree: a program that never prints a token does not need one,
//     and having one guarantees somebody eventually prints "just the safe part";
//   - no log file and no debug switch of any kind that could dump the header;
//   - no subprocess (so no token in argv) and no environment variable carrying a token. This file
//     imports no process-spawning module at all, and must keep importing none.

import fs from 'node:fs';
import {
  apiEndpoint, readCredentials,
  readAndPrunePendingReports, dropPendingReports,
  buildToolCallRpcBodies, parseRpcMessage, toolCallSucceeded,
  PENDING_REPORT_MAX,
} from '../scripts/lib/common.js';
import { accessTokenUsable, ensureFreshAccessToken, msUntil } from '../scripts/lib/credentials.js';

/** Recovery line for exit 1, needs-login case. It must name /kabo-login: this is the only text the stuck user gets. */
const NEEDS_LOGIN =
  'Kabo is not authorized on this machine. Run /kabo-login in Claude Code to sign in (a browser tab confirms a short code).';
/**
 * Recovery line for exit 1, endpoint-mismatch case. Deliberately distinct from NEEDS_LOGIN: the two
 * paths shared one sentence until 2026-08, and a user whose only problem was KABO_API_ENDPOINT was
 * told to sign in again - which succeeds, changes nothing, and loops. kabo-auth status comes first
 * because it is the one command that prints both endpoints and makes the mismatch visible.
 */
const WRONG_ENDPOINT =
  'Kabo is signed in for a different endpoint than this session expects. Run kabo-auth status for details, then /kabo-login to sign in again.';
/** Recovery line for exit 2. It must NOT send anyone through a login they do not need. */
const UNREACHABLE =
  'Could not reach the Kabo authorization server (network error or timeout). Your sign-in is still valid; retry in a moment.';
/**
 * Recovery line for exit 3, probe mode only: the access token is expired by the local clock and
 * the probe is forbidden from renewing it. Nothing is wrong - the next real request renews it - so
 * the line must NOT name /kabo-login (that substring is the hook's signature for a sign-in verdict)
 * and must not read like a network failure.
 */
const LOCALLY_EXPIRED =
  'Kabo credential present; the access token has expired locally and will be renewed on first use (the probe never renews).';
/**
 * Relay mode's own lines. None of them may contain `/kabo-login` (that substring is the hook's
 * signature for a sign-in verdict, and sign-in is the probe's job, not the relay's) and none of them
 * may name a skill, a path or anything else out of the buffer - the entries are attacker-influenced
 * text (skill_id comes from a package's unsigned top-level fields), and stderr is echoed by the hook
 * into the user's terminal. Counts only.
 */
const RELAY_REJECTED = 'Kabo could not relay this machine\'s buffered verification events: the server rejected the credential.';
const RELAY_UNAVAILABLE = 'Kabo could not relay this machine\'s buffered verification events (they stay queued for a later session).';
/** The tool takes {events: Event[]}; the name is the platform contract's, not this file's. */
const TELEMETRY_TOOL = 'telemetry_report_usage';
/** Refresh tokens expiring within this window get an informational line on exit 0 (probe mode only). */
const REFRESH_WARN_MS = 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;

/**
 * Blocking write to a file descriptor.
 *
 * process.stdout.write is asynchronous when stdout is a pipe - which it always is here, since the
 * host reads us - and process.exit() would then truncate the very line the host is waiting for. That
 * failure mode is intermittent and looks exactly like "the helper produced no header".
 */
function writeFd(fd, text) {
  const buf = Buffer.from(text, 'utf8');
  let offset = 0;
  while (offset < buf.length) {
    try {
      offset += fs.writeSync(fd, buf, offset);
    } catch (err) {
      if (err && err.code === 'EAGAIN') continue; // non-blocking pipe not ready yet
      return;
    }
  }
}

function fail(code, message = code === 1 ? NEEDS_LOGIN : UNREACHABLE) {
  writeFd(2, `${message}\n`);
  process.exit(code);
}

// Hard budget. The host allows 10s per helper invocation; a stuck renewal must not spend all of it on
// every MCP request. Being marked needs-auth is recoverable; a session where every tool call hangs for
// ten seconds is not. unref() so a fast path is never held open by this timer.
const budget = setTimeout(() => fail(2), 9500);
budget.unref();

try {
  const endpoint = apiEndpoint();

  // Mode first: relay mode has to be able to say nothing at all, and the credential checks below
  // speak (NEEDS_LOGIN, WRONG_ENDPOINT). SessionStart runs the relay **alongside** the probe, and
  // those two sentences are the probe's to say - printed twice they read like two problems.
  const probeMode = process.argv.includes('--probe');
  const relayMode = !probeMode && process.argv.includes('--relay');

  // Nothing queued is the overwhelmingly common case for the relay, and it must cost zero network,
  // zero credential reads and zero output. The read also prunes (TTL, cap, bad lines), which is
  // worth doing even when the relay itself never gets off the ground.
  if (relayMode && readAndPrunePendingReports().length === 0) process.exit(0);

  // Fast path first: roughly 98% of calls have a cached access token that is not close to expiry, and
  // those must cost zero network round trips.
  const cached = readCredentials();
  if (!cached) relayMode ? process.exit(1) : fail(1);
  if (cached.endpoint !== endpoint) relayMode ? process.exit(1) : fail(1, WRONG_ENDPOINT);

  // A dead refresh token means the next renewal is guaranteed to fail; there is nothing for the
  // relay to try. Silent for the same reason as above.
  if (relayMode && msUntil(cached.refresh_expires_at) <= 0) process.exit(1);

  if (probeMode) {
    // The refresh token's own expiry is read off the file, never sent anywhere. A dead refresh
    // token is the needs-login verdict no matter what the access token looks like: the next
    // renewal is guaranteed to fail. The default mode deletes the file in this state; the probe
    // only reports (it must never mutate the credential - see the renewal rule below).
    if (msUntil(cached.refresh_expires_at) <= 0) fail(1);
    const refreshLeft = msUntil(cached.refresh_expires_at);
    if (refreshLeft <= REFRESH_WARN_MS) {
      // Date only (no token bytes, no timestamps precise enough to fingerprint a login).
      const day = new Date(Date.now() + refreshLeft).toISOString().slice(0, 10);
      writeFd(2, `Kabo sign-in expires on ${day}; run /kabo-login before then to stay signed in.\n`);
    }
  }

  let accessToken;
  if (accessTokenUsable(cached)) {
    accessToken = cached.access_token;
  } else if (relayMode) {
    // Same rule as the probe (no rotation under an outer kill timeout), but silent and its own exit
    // code: the events simply wait for a session whose access token is fresh.
    process.exit(3);
  } else if (probeMode) {
    // Probe mode NEVER renews. A renewal is a strict single-use rotation, and the probe's caller
    // (SessionStart) enforces its own outer kill timeout - a rotation truncated between the
    // server consuming the old refresh token and this process persisting the new one burns the
    // credential, which is the very accident the probe exists to prevent. A locally stale access
    // token also is not the probe's question: the question is "does the server still accept a
    // credential that LOOKS fresh here", and a stale one will be renewed by the next default-mode
    // call anyway. Exit 3 with its own sentence: this is a present, expected-to-work credential,
    // and the hook must be able to say so rather than lumping it with "unreachable" (exit 2).
    fail(3, LOCALLY_EXPIRED);
  } else {
    const resolved = await ensureFreshAccessToken(endpoint);
    if (resolved.status === 'needs_login') fail(1);
    if (resolved.status !== 'ok') fail(2);
    accessToken = resolved.access_token;
  }

  if (probeMode) {
    // One POST against the MCP path this credential exists for. Auth runs before body parsing
    // there, so the status splits cleanly: 401 = the server no longer accepts this credential
    // (exit 1, needs-login sentence); any other HTTP status = the credential passed the gate
    // (exit 0 - the request body being a non-MCP no-op is irrelevant); no response = exit 2.
    // The timeout is deliberately shorter than the 9.5s budget: SessionStart runs this in
    // parallel with its other requests and treats a slow probe as "could not tell", never as
    // a sign-out.
    try {
      const response = await fetch(`${endpoint}/mcp-for-claude`, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}`, 'content-type': 'application/json' },
        body: '{}',
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(3500),
      });
      if (response.status === 401) fail(1);
      writeFd(2, 'Kabo sign-in accepted by the server.\n');
      process.exit(0);
    } catch {
      fail(2);
    }
  }

  if (relayMode) {
    // Re-read rather than reusing the earlier read: the emptiness check above happened before the
    // credential work, and this is the batch the prune below has to match exactly.
    const batch = readAndPrunePendingReports().slice(-PENDING_REPORT_MAX);
    if (batch.length === 0) process.exit(0);
    const [initBody, callBody] = buildToolCallRpcBodies(TELEMETRY_TOOL, { events: batch });
    const post = async (body, id) => {
      const response = await fetch(`${endpoint}/mcp-for-claude`, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {
          Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}`,
          'content-type': 'application/json',
          accept: 'application/json, text/event-stream',
        },
        body: JSON.stringify(body),
        // Half the probe's per-request budget, because the relay spends two round trips and shares
        // the same outer 4.5s kill timeout in SessionStart. A relay that does not fit costs nothing:
        // the entries stay queued for the next session.
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(2000),
      });
      return { status: response.status, message: parseRpcMessage(await response.text(), id) };
    };
    try {
      const init = await post(initBody, 1);
      // 401/403 is the one status worth short-circuiting on - retrying the second POST with the
      // same rejected credential cannot go better. Any other non-2xx initialize is NOT fatal: the
      // server is stateless, so tools/call carries its own verdict.
      if (init.status === 401 || init.status === 403) fail(1, RELAY_REJECTED);
      const call = await post(callBody, 2);
      if (call.status === 401 || call.status === 403) fail(1, RELAY_REJECTED);
      if (call.status < 200 || call.status >= 300) fail(2, RELAY_UNAVAILABLE);
      // A tool-level failure arrives as result.isError, not as a JSON-RPC error. Reading that as
      // success is precisely how a buffer gets pruned while the server took nothing.
      if (!toolCallSucceeded(call.message)) fail(2, RELAY_UNAVAILABLE);
      // Confirmed: the batch is in (`accepted`) or was already in (`duplicates`) - both mean the
      // platform holds these events and re-sending them is pure waste. Only these ids are dropped,
      // so anything appended meanwhile survives.
      const dropped = dropPendingReports(batch.map((entry) => entry.event_id));
      writeFd(2, `Kabo relayed ${batch.length} buffered verification event(s) (${dropped} cleared from the local queue).\n`);
      process.exit(0);
    } catch {
      fail(2, RELAY_UNAVAILABLE);
    }
  }

  writeFd(1, `${JSON.stringify({ Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}` })}\n`);
  process.exit(0);
} catch {
  // Anything unexpected is reported as a transport problem and never as a printed exception: a stack
  // trace is the one place a token could otherwise reach stderr.
  fail(2);
}
